# Handoff: Vercel Function Size Optimization — mirelle-site

## Project
Next.js 15.5.15 (Turbopack) blog/content site (`mirelleinspo.com`), deployed on Vercel.
~1,200+ MD/MDX content files under `src/content/` (~23MB total: blogs, shop, salons,
faqs, work-nails, trend-reports, spotlight, stories, topics, pinterest, nail-feed,
business, inspo).

## The problem (original)
Vercel Resources tab showed nearly every content page's serverless Function at
**12.3 MB**, driving up deployment/function cost. Root cause: static pages
(`dynamic = 'force-static'`, `revalidate: false`) call `lib/*.ts` helpers
(`getAllBlogPosts`, `getBlogPost`, etc.) that use `fs.readFileSync`/`readdirSync`
over `src/content/**` at module scope. Next.js's build tracer (`@vercel/nft`)
can't tell this only matters at build time, so it bundles the **entire**
`src/content` folder (~1,140+ files) into the deployed function for every page
that transitively imports these helpers — even though each page only ever
needs one file.

## Fix #1 (applied, real but incomplete): outputFileTracingExcludes
Added `outputFileTracingExcludes` entries in `next.config.ts` for every
statically-prerendered content route, telling Next.js to drop `src/content/**`
from those routes' function bundles. Deliberately NOT applied to routes that
genuinely need runtime fs access:
- `/blog` (force-dynamic listing page)
- `/api/search` (live cross-content search)
- `/api/blog/[slug]/pdf` (reads post per-request to render PDF)
- `/api/timeline/blog-cluster`, `/api/timeline/inspo-images` (runtime GET handlers)

**This did not work when first deployed** — user's live Resources tab (deployment
`qHXv9Fmq`) still showed 12.3MB on `/blog/*` pages AND on `/authors` (which had
had this exclusion applied even before this session started). Verified via
screenshot that the correct `next.config.ts` WAS present in that deployment's
Source tab — so it wasn't a "wrong file deployed" issue.

## Fix #2 (applied, root cause found via direct empirical testing)
Discovered — by actually building the project locally with the user's exact
build command (`next build --turbopack`) and inspecting the real
`.next/server/app/**/*.nft.json` trace files — that **the literal bracket
route-key syntax Next.js's own docs show (e.g. `"/authors/[slug]"`) is silently
ignored by the tracer for dynamic/parameterized routes on this Next.js +
Turbopack combination.** Plain non-parameterized routes (`/authors`, `/business`)
excluded correctly; every `[slug]`/`[...slug]`/`[design]`/`[city]` route did not,
despite the key being 100% correctly present in `required-server-files.json`.

**Verified fix, isolated A/B tested twice on the real repo:**
- Config with `"/authors/[slug]": [...]` only → `/authors/[slug]/page.js.nft.json`
  still contains 1,140 `src/content` files (bug reproduced).
- Config with `"/authors/*": [...]` only → same route drops to 0 `src/content`
  files (fix confirmed).

Applied `*` wildcard in place of every literal bracket segment across all 14
dynamic content route families. Full clean rebuild (`rm -rf .next && next build
--turbopack`) confirmed **all 14 routes + homepage now trace 0 files under
src/content**, while `/api/search` (intentionally untouched) still correctly
traces ~1,038 content files, proving the fix is precise, not a blanket exclude.

Corrected `next.config.ts` was given to the user (`/mnt/user-data/outputs/next.config.ts`
in that session — user needs to re-supply if starting fresh with new agent, or
it should be in their repo already if they committed it).

